1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:07,471
Your repository hasn't
received a commit in a week.

2
00:00:07,511 --> 00:00:12,431
Your workflow file hasn't changed in two
years. This morning your job runs code that

3
00:00:12,471 --> 00:00:16,978
didn't exist yesterday, with your job's
token and every secret the job holds.

4
00:00:17,318 --> 00:00:21,097
Nobody touched your repository.
Somebody moved a tag in another one.

5
00:00:21,137 --> 00:00:25,198
That somebody was either a maintainer
of the action or a person holding a

6
00:00:25,238 --> 00:00:29,243
maintainer's token, and from your side
of the line you can't tell which.

7
00:00:29,583 --> 00:00:34,157
You'll reproduce the move locally in three
commands, with a bare repository playing

8
00:00:34,197 --> 00:00:38,493
the action's repository. A bare repository
is one with no working tree, like a

9
00:00:38,533 --> 00:00:42,884
server's. Then you'll see the one form
of that line that wouldn't have changed.

10
00:00:42,924 --> 00:00:46,220
Keep that promise in mind. You'll
collect on it in the demo.

11
00:00:48,060 --> 00:00:51,810
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:51,850 --> 00:00:55,041
This is part five of the
model: third-party actions.

13
00:00:55,081 --> 00:00:59,887
An action is a packaged, reusable step
that a workflow calls with a uses line.

14
00:00:59,927 --> 00:01:03,490
It downloads and runs someone
else's code inside your job.

15
00:01:03,830 --> 00:01:07,477
Of the five parts, this is the one
that rests most directly on Git.

16
00:01:07,517 --> 00:01:12,098
The chapter said at the start that Git
contributes exactly two facts to the security

17
00:01:12,138 --> 00:01:16,280
model. A tag is a ref, a name that holds
a commit's ID, and it can be moved.

18
00:01:16,320 --> 00:01:20,902
A commit ID names content that can't change.
Both are things you've known since Part

19
00:01:20,942 --> 00:01:25,413
1 and Part 4 of this course. Here they
decide what code runs next to your secrets.

20
00:01:25,753 --> 00:01:30,793
Three topics. What a uses line trusts. How
to write a pin, how to keep it current,

21
00:01:30,833 --> 00:01:35,996
and what a pin doesn't protect against. And
organization policies: the settings that

22
00:01:36,036 --> 00:01:40,641
move some of these rules from "every
author must remember" to "the platform

23
00:01:40,681 --> 00:01:41,199
refuses".

24
00:01:41,539 --> 00:01:46,266
The demonstration is Git, not GitHub Actions,
which is why it can be shown locally.

25
00:01:48,106 --> 00:01:49,684
Here's what you'll walk away with.

26
00:01:50,024 --> 00:01:51,436
After this video you can:

27
00:01:51,776 --> 00:01:55,238
explain what uses: owner/action@v4 trusts;

28
00:01:55,578 --> 00:01:59,395
show locally that a tag can be moved to
another commit without any change in the

29
00:01:59,435 --> 00:02:00,504
workflow that names it;

30
00:02:00,844 --> 00:02:03,868
pin an action to a full commit
ID and keep the pin current;

31
00:02:04,208 --> 00:02:06,896
name three things pinning
does not protect against;

32
00:02:07,236 --> 00:02:10,893
say what organization policies
for allowed actions can enforce.

33
00:02:12,733 --> 00:02:17,399
In one sentence. uses with an owner, a
repository and a ref downloads and runs

34
00:02:17,439 --> 00:02:22,467
someone else's code inside your job, and
only a full commit ID guarantees that it is

35
00:02:22,507 --> 00:02:23,795
the code you reviewed.

36
00:02:24,135 --> 00:02:28,847
Precisely. From GitHub's secure use
reference. A compromised action "would have

37
00:02:28,887 --> 00:02:33,599
access to all secrets configured on your
repository, and may be able to use the

38
00:02:33,639 --> 00:02:38,532
GITHUB_TOKEN to write to the repository".
And: "Pinning an action to a full-length

39
00:02:38,572 --> 00:02:43,284
commit SHA is currently the only way to
use an action as an immutable release."

40
00:02:43,624 --> 00:02:47,940
A commit SHA is the commit ID, and
immutable means it can never change.

41
00:02:47,980 --> 00:02:52,971
Tags are a matter of trust: "Pin actions
to a tag only if you trust the creator… a

42
00:02:53,011 --> 00:02:57,573
tag can be moved or deleted if a bad
actor gains access to the repository."

43
00:02:57,613 --> 00:02:59,966
The same applies to reusable workflows.

44
00:03:00,306 --> 00:03:04,655
Quick quiz. Your workflow says
@v4, and the file never changes.

45
00:03:04,695 --> 00:03:09,323
When is that tag looked up? A, once,
on the day you wrote the line.

46
00:03:09,363 --> 00:03:11,762
B, again on every run. Your answer?

47
00:03:16,602 --> 00:03:17,935
B, on every run.

48
00:03:18,275 --> 00:03:22,856
So what does @v4 trust? Three things
at once. That the author is honest.

49
00:03:22,896 --> 00:03:26,258
That nobody else can push
to the author's repository.

50
00:03:26,298 --> 00:03:31,071
And that both stay true on every future
day your workflow runs, because the

51
00:03:31,111 --> 00:03:33,702
reference is resolved again on every run.

52
00:03:34,042 --> 00:03:38,423
Writing a pin. Take the commit ID
from the action's own repository.

53
00:03:38,463 --> 00:03:43,767
The reference adds a check: verify that
it's from the action's repository and not

54
00:03:43,807 --> 00:03:49,112
from a fork of it. The course's pin file
shows the command, git ls-remote --tags,

55
00:03:49,152 --> 00:03:50,497
which needs no login.

56
00:03:50,837 --> 00:03:54,980
Maintaining a pin. Pins don't update
themselves, and that's the point.

57
00:03:55,020 --> 00:03:59,700
So you let Dependabot, GitHub's dependency
update feature, propose the updates.

58
00:04:04,540 --> 00:04:09,694
That's the documented configuration. And
one behavior to know: since the fourteenth

59
00:04:09,734 --> 00:04:14,888
of July 2026, version updates wait a default
cooldown of three days after a release

60
00:04:14,928 --> 00:04:19,956
before a pull request is opened. Security
updates are immediate, and the cooldown

61
00:04:19,996 --> 00:04:25,212
option configures it. The textbook's reading:
a cooldown gives the ecosystem time to

62
00:04:25,252 --> 00:04:28,153
notice a malicious release
before you adopt it.

63
00:04:28,493 --> 00:04:32,845
The limits of pinning. The textbook says:
state these whenever you recommend it.

64
00:04:33,185 --> 00:04:38,237
First. A pin protects against a moved tag,
but, quoting the Phase 0 report, "not

65
00:04:38,277 --> 00:04:43,394
against a malicious commit pinned deliberately
or against unpinned actions nested

66
00:04:43,434 --> 00:04:48,422
inside composite actions". A composite
action is an action that bundles several

67
00:04:48,462 --> 00:04:51,032
steps. So: read the diff of a pin update.

68
00:04:51,372 --> 00:04:56,276
Second. Quoting GitHub: "Dependabot only
creates alerts for vulnerable actions that

69
00:04:56,316 --> 00:05:00,803
use semantic versioning and will not
create alerts for actions pinned to SHA

70
00:05:00,843 --> 00:05:04,257
values." Pinning trades automatic
alerts for immutability.

71
00:05:04,297 --> 00:05:08,069
Version updates, and advisories
you read yourself, fill the gap.

72
00:05:08,409 --> 00:05:13,204
Third. A remote script fetched and
executed in a run step, the pattern of

73
00:05:13,244 --> 00:05:18,568
downloading with curl and piping into a
shell, is the same risk class with no pin

74
00:05:18,608 --> 00:05:21,746
available. That's the
Codecov case of video 162.

75
00:05:22,086 --> 00:05:28,048
Fourth, a nuance. Immutable releases,
generally available since the twenty-eighth of

76
00:05:28,088 --> 00:05:33,977
October 2025, make a published release's
tag unchangeable once a maintainer enables

77
00:05:34,017 --> 00:05:38,693
the feature. astral-sh/setup-uv
has used them since version 8.0.0.

78
00:05:38,733 --> 00:05:43,765
But you can't see from a uses line
whether a tag is protected this way.

79
00:05:43,805 --> 00:05:45,766
So the pin remains the rule.

80
00:05:46,106 --> 00:05:51,317
Organization policies. In one sentence:
policies move three of this chapter's rules

81
00:05:51,357 --> 00:05:55,113
from "every author must remember"
to "the platform refuses".

82
00:05:55,453 --> 00:05:59,499
Allowed actions and reusable
workflows. The choices: allow all.

83
00:05:59,539 --> 00:06:02,937
Only those in the owner's
organization or enterprise.

84
00:06:02,977 --> 00:06:08,191
Or the owner plus a selection, with switches
for actions created by GitHub and by

85
00:06:08,231 --> 00:06:13,574
verified Marketplace creators, and a pattern
list. Available on all plans since the

86
00:06:13,614 --> 00:06:15,066
fifth of February 2026.

87
00:06:15,406 --> 00:06:21,036
Blocking. An entry prefixed with an
exclamation mark blocks a specific action or

88
00:06:21,076 --> 00:06:24,155
version. Since the
fifteenth of August 2025.

89
00:06:24,495 --> 00:06:28,059
"Require actions to be pinned
to a full-length commit SHA".

90
00:06:28,099 --> 00:06:33,008
With it, any workflow that attempts to use
an action that isn't pinned will fail,

91
00:06:33,048 --> 00:06:37,834
including GitHub's own actions. One
exception is stated: reusable workflows can

92
00:06:37,874 --> 00:06:39,484
still be referenced by tag.

93
00:06:39,824 --> 00:06:44,161
The workflow permissions default:
the read-only token of video 156.

94
00:06:44,201 --> 00:06:49,061
A restrictive organization default
disables the permissive option below it.

95
00:06:49,401 --> 00:06:54,142
Fork pull request approval: who needs
approval before a pull_request run starts.

96
00:06:54,482 --> 00:06:59,320
Workflow execution protections: actor
rules, for who may trigger workflows, and

97
00:06:59,360 --> 00:07:04,260
event rules, for which events may run.
They're built on rulesets, GitHub's named

98
00:07:04,300 --> 00:07:08,274
lists of rules, at enterprise,
organization and repository level.

99
00:07:08,614 --> 00:07:13,412
And "Allow GitHub Actions to create and
approve pull requests": off by default for

100
00:07:13,452 --> 00:07:18,131
new personal repositories. Keep it off,
so that a workflow can't approve its own

101
00:07:18,171 --> 00:07:18,544
change.

102
00:07:18,884 --> 00:07:24,051
The change dated 2 November 2026. With
execution protections, GitHub introduced a

103
00:07:24,091 --> 00:07:29,257
default event rule. For public repositories
that don't already have an applicable

104
00:07:29,297 --> 00:07:32,985
event policy, a default rule
disables pull_request_target.

105
00:07:33,025 --> 00:07:37,998
It initially runs in evaluate mode, and
GitHub announced that on the second of

106
00:07:38,038 --> 00:07:42,433
November 2026 it is enforced
automatically for affected repositories.

107
00:07:42,773 --> 00:07:47,624
That date lies after the course baseline,
so check the changelog on the day you

108
00:07:47,664 --> 00:07:51,462
watch this. Private and internal
repositories aren't affected.

109
00:07:51,502 --> 00:07:56,477
A maintainer can explicitly allow the
event, optionally for named workflow files.

110
00:07:56,517 --> 00:08:01,430
The textbook's advice for a public repository
with a labeler or a welcome bot on

111
00:08:01,470 --> 00:08:06,445
that trigger: decide before that date.
Migrate it, or allow it deliberately after

112
00:08:06,485 --> 00:08:08,612
reviewing it against section 21A.5.

113
00:08:08,952 --> 00:08:14,888
Policies do not replace review of the
files. The control for that is a code-owner

114
00:08:14,928 --> 00:08:20,863
rule on .github/workflows/, enforced by a
ruleset, as you set it up in video 136.

115
00:08:22,703 --> 00:08:27,286
Analogy, from the textbook. A tag is a
street address, and a commit ID is a

116
00:08:27,326 --> 00:08:32,094
fingerprint. Whoever owns the plot can
put up a different building at the same

117
00:08:32,134 --> 00:08:33,204
address overnight.

118
00:08:33,544 --> 00:08:36,614
The textbook says the analogy
breaks in your favor.

119
00:08:36,654 --> 00:08:39,969
A fingerprint can in principle
be shared by two people.

120
00:08:40,009 --> 00:08:44,848
Forging a commit ID means, in GitHub's
words, producing "a SHA-1 collision for a

121
00:08:44,888 --> 00:08:46,434
valid Git object payload".

122
00:08:46,774 --> 00:08:51,448
Keep both halves. An address tells you
where to go, and somebody else decides what

123
00:08:51,488 --> 00:08:56,221
stands there. A fingerprint tells you what
you'll find, and nobody can change that.

124
00:08:56,561 --> 00:09:01,148
Then add the limit, so that you don't
oversell it. The fingerprint identifies the

125
00:09:01,188 --> 00:09:04,690
building. It says nothing about
whether the building is sound.

126
00:09:04,730 --> 00:09:09,318
A pinned commit can be malicious if it was
pinned deliberately, and it can itself

127
00:09:09,358 --> 00:09:13,602
call other actions by tag. Pinning
answers one question: is this the code I

128
00:09:13,642 --> 00:09:16,916
reviewed? It doesn't answer:
was the code I reviewed good?

129
00:09:18,756 --> 00:09:23,833
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper.
Write one workflow line that ends in @v1.

130
00:09:23,873 --> 00:09:29,144
Draw a tag and two commits. Point the tag
at the first commit, then move its arrow

131
00:09:29,184 --> 00:09:32,771
to the second. What did you
change in the workflow line?

132
00:09:32,811 --> 00:09:33,807
Say it out loud.

133
00:09:46,547 --> 00:09:49,838
Nothing. The top line didn't
change, and what it runs did.

134
00:09:49,878 --> 00:09:54,663
The pinned line at the bottom can't change
what it runs without a commit to your own

135
00:09:54,703 --> 00:09:55,294
repository.

136
00:09:57,134 --> 00:10:00,107
Step 1: what a consumer
gets for @v1 today.

137
00:10:00,447 --> 00:10:05,729
git ls-remote is 🟢 SAFE: it reads. v1 is
an annotated tag: a tag ref that points at

138
00:10:05,769 --> 00:10:10,795
a tag object, which in turn names the
commit. How many lines will there be, and

139
00:10:10,835 --> 00:10:13,424
which one is the commit? Say it out loud.

140
00:10:18,264 --> 00:10:24,859
Two lines. The first is the tag object,
71c87ce. The second, ending in ^{}, is the

141
00:10:24,899 --> 00:10:31,575
commit it points at: 80827f4. That commit
is what a workflow saying @v1 runs today.

142
00:10:31,915 --> 00:10:35,099
Step 2: someone with push
access moves the tag.

143
00:10:35,439 --> 00:10:40,991
git tag -f is 🟡 CAUTION: it moves a
local tag, and a tag has no reflog.

144
00:10:41,331 --> 00:10:46,756
git push --force of a tag is 🔴 DANGEROUS,
so the five answers before it runs.

145
00:10:46,796 --> 00:10:52,434
What it changes: the remote tag ref. What
it can destroy: it can silently change

146
00:10:52,474 --> 00:10:58,395
what every consumer of the tag runs. How to
preview: git ls-remote --tags origin v1.

147
00:10:58,435 --> 00:11:03,576
How to recover: by pushing the old tag
object back, if you still have it.

148
00:11:03,616 --> 00:11:08,970
When it is appropriate: only for a
deliberate "moving major tag" policy that

149
00:11:09,010 --> 00:11:10,461
consumers know about.

150
00:11:10,801 --> 00:11:15,963
Here it is run in the sandbox, against a
local bare repository, to show you what the

151
00:11:16,003 --> 00:11:17,078
other side can do.

152
00:11:17,418 --> 00:11:23,321
"Updated tag v1, was 71c87ce". And the
push reports a forced update, with a plus

153
00:11:23,361 --> 00:11:29,265
sign: from 71c87ce to 0ca190d. In the
scenario, the person at this keyboard is a

154
00:11:29,305 --> 00:11:32,979
maintainer, or whoever
holds a maintainer's token.

155
00:11:33,319 --> 00:11:35,471
Step 3: the same question again.

156
00:11:35,811 --> 00:11:39,332
The name is the same. What do
you expect on the two lines?

157
00:11:39,372 --> 00:11:40,621
Make your prediction.

158
00:11:45,461 --> 00:11:49,855
A new tag object, 0ca190d,
and a new commit, 5de1e2d.

159
00:11:49,895 --> 00:11:54,707
Same name, different commit.
No consumer workflow changed.

160
00:11:54,747 --> 00:11:58,221
A workflow that says @v1 now runs 5de1e2d.

161
00:11:58,561 --> 00:12:03,526
Then the second half of the snippet. git
cat-file -p with the old commit's full ID

162
00:12:03,566 --> 00:12:05,174
and the path of action.yml.

163
00:12:05,514 --> 00:12:09,911
The commit that was reviewed still exists,
and its ID still names exactly that

164
00:12:09,951 --> 00:12:13,608
content. A workflow pinned to that
ID runs what it ran yesterday.

165
00:12:13,648 --> 00:12:18,329
There's the promise from the opening: the
one form of the line that doesn't change.

166
00:12:24,669 --> 00:12:29,207
Now how the course records pins. In
workflow 12, find the checkout step.

167
00:12:29,247 --> 00:12:34,039
The comment above it says: full commit
SHA, version as a comment, the format

168
00:12:34,079 --> 00:12:39,253
Dependabot maintains. The line itself is
the action, an at sign, forty characters,

169
00:12:39,293 --> 00:12:44,149
and the version after a hash sign. Every
uses line in the file has that form:

170
00:12:44,189 --> 00:12:48,854
checkout, the uv setup action, the
dependency review action, and the cloud

171
00:12:48,894 --> 00:12:50,062
credentials action.

172
00:12:50,402 --> 00:12:54,923
In the pin file, each action has one row:
the name, the version, the commit ID.

173
00:12:54,963 --> 00:12:59,600
The header says when the pins were read
and how. Read versions from that file, on

174
00:12:59,640 --> 00:13:04,277
the day, and re-verify a pin with the
command the file gives before you trust it.

175
00:13:06,117 --> 00:13:07,765
Five mistakes to watch for.

176
00:13:08,105 --> 00:13:13,432
Treating @v4 as a version. Root cause: it
is a tag, resolved again on every run, and

177
00:13:13,472 --> 00:13:18,352
a tag can be moved or deleted by whoever
can push to the action's repository.

178
00:13:18,692 --> 00:13:24,041
Taking the commit ID from a fork of the
action. Root cause: the pin is only as good

179
00:13:24,081 --> 00:13:29,234
as its source; the documentation says to
verify that the ID is from the action's

180
00:13:29,274 --> 00:13:31,636
repository and not a repository fork.

181
00:13:31,976 --> 00:13:36,566
Pinning and then never updating. Root
cause: pins do not update themselves, and

182
00:13:36,606 --> 00:13:41,020
Dependabot creates no vulnerability
alerts for actions pinned to commit IDs.

183
00:13:41,360 --> 00:13:45,925
Approving a pin update without reading its
diff. Root cause: a pin does not protect

184
00:13:45,965 --> 00:13:48,533
against a malicious commit
pinned deliberately.

185
00:13:48,873 --> 00:13:53,809
Believing a pinned workflow has no unpinned
code. Root cause: actions nested inside

186
00:13:53,849 --> 00:13:58,185
composite actions, and scripts fetched
in run steps, are outside the pin.

187
00:14:00,025 --> 00:14:05,020
Now, out of the lab. An ML platform
organization with sixty repositories decides to

188
00:14:05,060 --> 00:14:09,995
pin. Asking sixty teams to remember is the
first idea, and the textbook's sentence

189
00:14:10,035 --> 00:14:14,788
about policies describes why it won't
hold: you want the rule moved from "every

190
00:14:14,828 --> 00:14:17,701
author must remember" to
"the platform refuses".

191
00:14:18,041 --> 00:14:23,136
So the organization does three things. It
adds the github-actions ecosystem to the

192
00:14:23,176 --> 00:14:28,334
Dependabot configuration of every repository,
so that pins have a source of updates

193
00:14:28,374 --> 00:14:33,594
before the rule bites. It converts the
existing references, reading each ID from the

194
00:14:33,634 --> 00:14:38,792
action's own repository. Then it switches
on the policy that requires actions to be

195
00:14:38,832 --> 00:14:44,052
pinned to a full-length commit SHA. From
that day a workflow with an unpinned action

196
00:14:44,092 --> 00:14:44,428
fails.

197
00:14:44,768 --> 00:14:49,063
Two details from the table decide
whether the rollout goes smoothly.

198
00:14:49,103 --> 00:14:54,417
The policy applies to GitHub's own actions
as well, so actions/checkout by tag fails

199
00:14:54,457 --> 00:14:59,580
too. And reusable workflows can still be
referenced by tag, so the central deploy

200
00:14:59,620 --> 00:15:04,615
workflow that forty repositories call
needs its own discipline: a pinned ref by

201
00:15:04,655 --> 00:15:07,739
convention, and code-owner
review on the callers.

202
00:15:09,579 --> 00:15:15,485
Your turn. Do Exercise 29.5,
"What can this token do?", in

203
00:15:15,525 --> 00:15:19,381
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

204
00:15:19,721 --> 00:15:24,618
For each workflow in the exercise, before
you compute the token's scopes, list every

205
00:15:24,658 --> 00:15:29,438
uses line and mark it as pinned or floating.
Predict for each floating one what it

206
00:15:29,478 --> 00:15:32,376
could do with the token
you are about to work out.

207
00:15:32,716 --> 00:15:38,012
The challenge is Exercise 29.8, "A
workflow nobody wrote", in the same file.

208
00:15:39,852 --> 00:15:43,068
Read the question on screen.
Say your answer out loud.

209
00:15:47,908 --> 00:15:52,551
A strong answer first says, in one sentence,
what pinning does protect against, in

210
00:15:52,591 --> 00:15:57,063
Git terms. Then it gives three limits
that are different in kind, each with its

211
00:15:57,103 --> 00:16:01,802
reason: one about the pinned code itself,
one about code the pin doesn't reach, and

212
00:16:01,842 --> 00:16:04,886
one about what you give up
in return for immutability.

213
00:16:04,926 --> 00:16:09,398
The follow-up asks how you keep pins
current without re-opening the window that

214
00:16:09,438 --> 00:16:14,081
pinning closed. Think about who proposes
the update, how long it waits, and what a

215
00:16:14,121 --> 00:16:15,737
human does before merging it.

216
00:16:17,577 --> 00:16:18,504
Let's land this.

217
00:16:18,844 --> 00:16:20,504
You should now be able to say:

218
00:16:20,844 --> 00:16:25,969
A uses line runs someone else's code with
your job's token and secrets, and a tag in

219
00:16:26,009 --> 00:16:28,060
it is resolved again on every run.

220
00:16:28,400 --> 00:16:33,503
A tag is a movable ref; a full commit ID
names content that cannot change, and it is

221
00:16:33,543 --> 00:16:36,320
the only immutable way
to reference an action.

222
00:16:36,660 --> 00:16:41,211
A pin is the commit ID with the version
as a comment, taken from the action's own

223
00:16:41,251 --> 00:16:45,518
repository and updated by Dependabot,
with a default cooldown of three days.

224
00:16:45,858 --> 00:16:50,386
Pinning does not vouch for the pinned code,
does not cover nested unpinned actions

225
00:16:50,426 --> 00:16:53,395
or downloaded scripts, and
gives up Dependabot alerts.

226
00:16:53,735 --> 00:16:58,662
Organization policies can restrict which
actions run and require pinning; reviewing

227
00:16:58,702 --> 00:17:02,073
the workflow files still needs
code owners and a ruleset.

228
00:17:03,913 --> 00:17:09,072
Read sections 21A.7 and
21A.8 of Chapter 21A.

229
00:17:09,412 --> 00:17:14,578
Today you moved a tag with three commands
and watched a commit ID stay exactly where

230
00:17:14,618 --> 00:17:19,723
it was. Replay that demo once in the lab.
Next time: secrets, and why masking isn't

231
00:17:19,763 --> 00:17:23,690
a boundary. Until then, look at
the state first and type second.

232
00:17:23,730 --> 00:17:25,178
See you in the next one.
